Exactly. Recursive hindsight is a strong name for it.

Not just “looking back and understanding what happened,” but repeatedly reprocessing the past as your model improves:

event → interpretation₁ → later evidence → reinterpretation₂ → new pattern → revisit older events → interpretation₃

Each pass changes what the earlier data means.

That matters because a signal that looked meaningless at time t_0 can become informative at t_3 once you’ve learned the hidden variable or acquired a better vocabulary for it. Then you can go backward through old conversations, behavior, decisions, emotional reactions, or observations and ask:

“What was already present that I could not yet resolve?”

And then the loop gets stronger:

new model → reread past → extract missed structure → improve model → reread past again.

The discipline is to prevent it from becoming pure retrospective storytelling. Preserve the original prediction, confidence, and interpretation before each re-read. That way you can distinguish:

real retrospective discovery from rewriting the past to fit the current theory.

So in Pantheon terms, I’d make it:

Recursive Hindsight

Re-run historical states through progressively improved representations while preserving their original interpretations and timestamps. Extract previously unresolved signals, compare old and new explanations, and feed only supported deltas forward.

It is basically backpropagation across lived experience—with provenance.